WEBVTT

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:17.140
Oh, hey Globie.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.330
You're probably wondering why I'm wearing all these streamers.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:23.340
Well, I want to find out how aerodynamic I am.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:24.800
Turn on the fan, Charlie.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.970
See, aerodynamics is the study of how

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:36.090
efficiently air flows around an object, like an airplane or a car or, well, me.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:41.140
All right, Tony, that's enough. Turn it off. Turn it off.

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:48.870
One of the best ways for scientists to study airflow around objects is in a wind tunnel.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:54.570
A wind tunnel is an enclosed space where the airflow over an object can be carefully controlled and studied.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:59.610
There are four types of wind tunnels, subsonic, transonic,

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:06.200
supersonic and hypersonic. The NASA Langley Research Center has all of these types of

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.270
tunnels and is one of the most extensive wind tunnel facilities in the world. Each type of

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:15.450
wind tunnel is used to study an object in a different wind speed. These wind speeds

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:21.400
are based on the speed of sound, which is about 1,235 kilometers per hour, depending

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:27.750
on altitude and air temperature. In warmer air, sound travels even faster. So the speed

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:29.970
of sound is how fast sound waves travel through

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.930
the air. For example, if I were standing in a field and I saw a lightning bolt 1.6

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:38.950
kilometers away, it would take five seconds from the time I saw the flash to

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:43.330
hear the thunder. The thunder is traveling at the speed of sound or Mach 1.

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:55.160
What? I was just tying my shoe. Wind tunnels measure airflow or how fast the

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.480
air is moving using the same scale used for the speed of sound.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.270
Hypersonic wind tunnels are used to study the airflow around objects that are slower than the speed of sound, like cars and commercial jets.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:11.140
Transonic wind tunnels are used to study aircraft that can fly around the speed of sound.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.490
Supersonic and hypersonic tunnels are used to study flight beyond the speed of sound.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:22.420
In fact, hypersonic wind tunnels can study flight up to seven times the speed of sound.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:27.500
But how do the engineers at NASA get a plane to move that fast in an enclosed area?

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:28.610
They don't.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:29.980
The object they're taking

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.800
Testing stays still, and the air moves instead.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:40.770
We use wind tunnels to provide information on the aerodynamics of various types of vehicles.

00:02:40.990 --> 00:02:43.410
We can do that in a safe way.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:45.670
Very controlled conditions in the wind tunnel.

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:49.890
We don't have to build a full-size airplane and risk a pilot's life.

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:55.040
We always test new configurations in a wind tunnel first, make sure they're safe,

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:59.970
understand their characteristics, and then we move on to building a full-size airplane.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.460
So different types of wind tunnels are used to test different effects wind has on aircrafts flying through the air.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:18.240
For example, the NASA Langley 14 by 22 foot subsonic tunnel is used to test certain objects that travel at less than Mach 1, or less than the speed of sound.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:23.890
The 14 by 22 is mainly designed to test low speed performance of aircrafts.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:29.910
Low speed testing is important to airplanes in that it's very important to safely take off and land.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:38.030
We consider takeoff and landing the most important part of our flight because if you don't take off and land safely, the rest of the flight doesn't mean anything.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:42.580
On the other hand, we also test other things besides aircraft in our facility.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:51.230
We test structures to see how wind flows around it, anywhere from large buildings to radar structures.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.450
We also test vehicles such as Humvees.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.960
And recently we just got into testing motorsports such as NASCARs.

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:06.330
and in the racers, and we also touched parachutes as well as a wheelchair in 1979.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:13.080
The NASA National Transonic Facility uses nitrogen gas to study the flow over wing sections

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:13.860
and other models.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.660
Because this wind tunnel is transonic, it's used to test objects that are meant to fly

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.120
at speeds around Mach 1.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.040
Just as sound moves faster in warmer air, warmer temperatures in the wind tunnel make

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.080
the air move faster.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:29.910
The Langley 8-foot-high temperature tunnel

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.800
is a hypersonic tunnel that uses superheated gas

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.040
to simulate speeds up to Mach 7.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.380
That's seven times the speed of sound.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:41.000
NASA even has a wind tunnel that's built just to study

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.220
how airflow affects aircraft that are spinning out of control.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.800
Whoa, that makes Globie and me dizzy.

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:50.440
Go on, Globie.

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.730
You know, Globie, the wind tunnels at NASA Langley Research Center

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:59.210
are hoping to make air travel even safer and a lot faster.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:04:59.960
And you want to...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.990
believe some of the other things that are tested in NASA's wind tunnels. Cars and

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:08.610
boats and even the material for Olympic swimsuits. Leave it to NASA to find a way

00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.710
to test aerodynamics without even stepping outside.
